@subject acffa2
@chapter 11
@title Payables, Provisions and Contingent Liabilities
@lo D6
@topic t1 | Trade payables | D6
@topic t2 | Provisions (IAS 37) | D6
@topic t3 | Contingencies | D6
@topic t4 | Reporting provisions | D6

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Under IAS 37, what is a provision?
+ A liability of uncertain timing or amount || Core definition.
- Any expense paid in advance || A prepayment.
- A possible obligation that is only disclosed || A contingent liability.
- An allowance against receivables || Different item.
explain: A provision is recognised in the SOFP.
mistake: Confusing provisions and contingent liabilities.
tip: Uncertain timing or amount.
src: Study text ch11 - provision (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which is NOT one of the three conditions for recognising a provision?
+ The amount must be known exactly || Only a reliable estimate is needed.
- A present obligation from a past event || A condition.
- A probable outflow of economic benefits || A condition.
- A reliable estimate of the amount || A condition.
explain: Estimates are acceptable.
mistake: Requiring certainty.
tip: Obligation, probable, estimate.
src: Study text ch11 - recognition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How should a POSSIBLE (not probable) contingent liability be treated?
+ Disclosed in a note || Not recognised.
- Recognised as a provision || Needs probable outflow.
- Ignored || Only if remote.
- Recognised as an asset || Not an asset.
explain: Remote contingent liabilities are ignored.
mistake: Recognising possible liabilities.
tip: Possible = disclose.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How is a PROBABLE contingent asset treated?
+ Disclosed in a note, but not recognised || Prudent treatment.
- Recognised as an asset || Only if virtually certain.
- Ignored || Probable assets are disclosed.
- Recognised as income || Not until virtually certain.
explain: Possible contingent assets are ignored.
mistake: Recognising probable assets.
tip: Assets need virtual certainty.
src: Study text ch11 - contingent assets (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is a constructive obligation?
+ An obligation created by the entity's past practice or public statements that others validly expect it to honour || Not legally required.
- An obligation under a written contract || Legal obligation.
- An obligation imposed by tax law || Legal obligation.
- An obligation that is remote || Not the meaning.
explain: Example: a published refund policy beyond legal requirements.
mistake: Thinking only legal duties count.
tip: Created by behaviour.
src: Study text ch11 - constructive obligation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the double entry to set up a provision?
+ Debit expense; credit provision || Expense up, liability up.
- Debit provision; credit expense || Reversed.
- Debit bank; credit provision || No cash.
- Debit provision; credit bank || That would be using it.
explain: Only the movement is charged in later years.
mistake: Debiting the provision.
tip: Provision = credit.
src: Study text ch11 - entry (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A warranty provision was $8,000 at the start of the year. The required provision at the year end is $11,000. What is charged ($) to profit or loss?
answer: 3000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Charge = closing provision - opening provision
step: 11,000 - 8,000.
step: = $3,000 increase.
check: 11000-8000
explain: Entry: Dr expense 3,000; Cr provision 3,000.
mistake: Charging the full $11,000.
tip: Charge the movement.
src: Study text ch11 - movement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: How should a REMOTE contingent liability be treated?
+ Ignored - no recognition or disclosure || Too unlikely.
- Disclosed in a note || That is for possible.
- Recognised as a provision || Not probable.
- Recognised as an asset || Not relevant.
explain: Disclosure would clutter the accounts.
mistake: Disclosing remote items.
tip: Remote = ignore.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which item is DEBITED to the trade payables account?
+ Cash paid to suppliers || Reduces the liability.
- Credit purchases || Credited.
- Opening balance || Credit b/f.
- Interest charged by suppliers || Increases liability.
explain: Returns, discounts received and contras are also debits.
mistake: Debiting purchases.
tip: Reductions = debits.
src: Study text ch11 - payables account (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Can a company make a provision for expected future operating losses?
+ No - there is no present obligation from a past event || IAS 37 prohibits it.
- Yes - always || Not allowed.
- Yes - if the losses are probable || Still no obligation.
- Only if the board approves || Not allowed.
explain: Provisions require a past obligating event.
mistake: Providing for future losses.
tip: No past event, no provision.
src: Study text ch11 - future losses (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Trade payables: b/f $12,000; credit purchases $90,000; cash paid $85,000; purchases returns $2,000; discounts received $1,000; contra with receivables $1,500.
q: What is the closing trade payables balance ($)?
answer: 12500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: C/f = b/f + purchases - payments - returns - discounts - contras
step: 12,000 + 90,000 = 102,000.
step: 102,000 - 85,000 - 2,000 - 1,000 - 1,500 = $12,500.
check: 12000+90000-85000-2000-1000-1500
explain: All reductions are debits.
mistake: Adding returns.
tip: Reductions out.
src: Study text ch11 - payables account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company sold 1,000 units with a one-year warranty. Past experience: 5% need minor repairs costing $40 each and 2% need major repairs costing $200 each. What warranty provision ($) is required?
answer: 6000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Provision = Σ(units x % x cost)
step: Minor: 1,000 x 5% x 40 = 2,000.
step: Major: 1,000 x 2% x 200 = 4,000; total = $6,000.
check: 1000*0.05*40+1000*0.02*200
explain: Past experience provides a reliable estimate.
mistake: Using only one category.
tip: Expected cost of all claims.
src: Study text ch11 - warranty (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mcq
level: S
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A customer sues a company. The lawyers say the company will PROBABLY lose and estimate damages of $50,000. How should this be treated?
+ Recognise a provision of $50,000 || Probable outflow, reliable estimate.
- Disclose a contingent liability only || Outflow is probable.
- Ignore it until the case ends || Must provide.
- Recognise a contingent asset || Not an asset.
explain: A present obligation exists from the past event.
mistake: Only disclosing probable losses.
tip: Probable = provide.
src: Study text ch11 - legal claim (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q014
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO create an obligation that may require a provision?
+ A legal requirement to clean up land contaminated by the company || Legal obligation.
+ A long-standing published policy of refunding unhappy customers, beyond legal requirements || Constructive obligation.
- A plan to buy new machinery next year || No obligation.
- Expected losses next year || No past event.
explain: Obligations can be legal or constructive.
mistake: Providing for intentions.
tip: Past event + no realistic alternative.
src: Study text ch11 - obligations (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each probability with the treatment of a contingent LIABILITY.
pair: Virtually certain => Recognise a liability
pair: Probable => Recognise a provision
pair: Possible => Disclose in a note
pair: Remote => Ignore
explain: Contingent assets need virtual certainty to be recognised.
mistake: Disclosing remote items.
tip: Probability drives treatment.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: The opening provision for legal claims is $8,000. At the year end, the required provision is $5,500. What is the effect ($) on profit? (Enter the credit as a positive number.)
answer: 2500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Movement = opening - closing
step: 8,000 - 5,500.
step: = $2,500 credited to profit or loss.
check: 8000-5500
explain: Entry: Dr provision; Cr expense.
mistake: Charging the closing provision.
tip: Smaller provision = profit up.
src: Study text ch11 - decrease (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: A company is suing a supplier, and its lawyers say success is PROBABLE but not virtually certain. How is this treated?
+ Disclose a contingent asset in a note || Not recognised.
- Recognise an asset and income || Needs virtual certainty.
- Ignore it || Probable assets are disclosed.
- Recognise a provision || Not a liability.
explain: Possible contingent assets are ignored.
mistake: Recognising probable assets.
tip: Assets: probable = disclose.
src: Study text ch11 - contingent assets (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is a LEGAL obligation?
+ An obligation arising from a contract, legislation or other operation of law || Enforceable.
- An obligation created by past practice || Constructive.
- A future intention to spend || Not an obligation.
- A remote possibility || Not an obligation.
explain: Both legal and constructive obligations can lead to provisions.
mistake: Thinking only legal obligations count.
tip: Law or contract.
src: Study text ch11 - legal obligation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Payables b/f $13,000; payables c/f $15,000; cash paid to suppliers $70,000; purchases returns $1,200.
q: What were credit purchases ($) for the year?
answer: 73200
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Purchases = c/f + payments + returns - b/f
step: 15,000 + 70,000 + 1,200 = 86,200.
step: 86,200 - 13,000 = $73,200.
check: 15000+70000+1200-13000
explain: Purchases is the balancing figure.
mistake: Omitting returns.
tip: Rebuild the account.
src: Study text ch11 - working back (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How is a provision expected to be settled in 3 years presented?
+ As a non-current liability || Timing decides classification.
- As a current liability || Due after 12 months.
- As equity || Not equity.
- In a note only || It is recognised.
explain: Notes disclose the nature and movement.
mistake: Calling all provisions current.
tip: Over 12 months = non-current.
src: Study text ch11 - presentation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee recognises a $20,000 provision for a claim the lawyers describe as "possible but unlikely to succeed". What is correct?
+ Disclose a contingent liability; do not recognise a provision || Outflow is not probable.
- Keep the provision || Not probable.
- Ignore it completely || Possible = disclose.
- Recognise a contingent asset || Not an asset.
explain: Recognition needs a probable outflow.
mistake: Providing for possible outflows.
tip: Possible = note.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What does "probable" mean in IAS 37?
+ More likely than not || Over 50%.
- Certain || Too strong.
- Any chance at all || Too weak.
- Less than 10% || Remote.
explain: Virtually certain is much stronger than probable.
mistake: Equating probable with certain.
tip: Probable > 50%.
src: Study text ch11 - probability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Put these probability levels in order from MOST to LEAST likely.
item: Virtually certain
item: Probable
item: Possible
item: Remote
explain: Each level has a different accounting treatment.
mistake: Placing possible above probable.
tip: Certain, probable, possible, remote.
src: Study text ch11 - probability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Which is disclosed about provisions in the notes?
+ The nature of the obligation and the movement during the year || Required.
- The names of all customers || Not required.
- The company's share price || Not required.
- Nothing - they are on the face only || Notes are required.
explain: Uncertainties about timing and amount are also explained.
mistake: Omitting movement details.
tip: Nature + movement.
src: Study text ch11 - disclosure (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which BEST describes a trade payable?
+ An amount owed to a supplier for goods or services bought on credit || Current liability.
- An amount owed by a customer || Receivable.
- A provision for a lawsuit || Provision.
- A bank loan || Borrowing.
explain: Trade payables are known in amount and timing.
mistake: Calling payables provisions.
tip: Owe a supplier = payable.
src: Study text ch11 - payables (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A retailer has a policy of refunding faulty goods. Sales were $500,000 and past experience shows refunds average 1.5% of sales. What refund provision ($) is required?
answer: 7500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Provision = sales x expected refund %
step: 500,000 x 1.5%.
step: = $7,500.
check: 500000*0.015
explain: The policy creates a constructive obligation.
mistake: Not providing because it is not legally required.
tip: Policy = obligation.
src: Study text ch11 - refunds (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the difference between a provision and an accrual?
+ A provision has more uncertainty about timing or amount than an accrual || Accruals are usually more certain.
- Accruals are assets; provisions are liabilities || Both are liabilities.
- Provisions are never recognised || They are.
- There is no difference || There is.
explain: Accruals are typically for goods or services received but not invoiced.
mistake: Treating them as identical.
tip: Uncertainty marks a provision.
src: Study text ch11 - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee recognises a $40,000 asset for an insurance claim the company is "likely" to win. What is correct?
+ Disclose a contingent asset; recognise only when virtually certain || Probable is not enough.
- Recognise the asset as the trainee did || Too early.
- Ignore it || Probable assets are disclosed.
- Recognise a provision || Not a liability.
explain: Prudence treats gains more strictly than losses.
mistake: Recognising probable gains.
tip: Gains need virtual certainty.
src: Study text ch11 - contingent assets (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: A warranty provision was $15,000 at the start of the year. During the year claims of $9,000 were paid and charged against the provision. The required closing provision is $18,000.
q: What is charged ($) to profit or loss for warranties this year?
answer: 12000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Charge = closing - (opening - amounts used)
step: Provision after use = 15,000 - 9,000 = 6,000.
step: Charge = 18,000 - 6,000 = $12,000.
check: 18000-(15000-9000)
explain: Payments made against the provision are not a new expense.
mistake: Expensing claims and the full movement.
tip: Use, then top up.
src: Study text ch11 - provision movement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the "past event" for a warranty provision?
+ The sale of goods under warranty || The obligation arises at sale.
- A future customer complaint || Not past.
- The preparation of the accounts || Not the event.
- The purchase of raw materials || Not the event.
explain: Claims in future result from sales already made.
mistake: Waiting for claims.
tip: Sale creates the duty.
src: Study text ch11 - obligating event (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A company announces publicly, before the year end, a detailed plan to close a factory and has informed staff and unions. Does it have an obligation at the year end?
+ Yes - a constructive obligation, as it has created a valid expectation in those affected || Announced plan.
- No - only a board decision exists || It has been announced.
- No - closures never create obligations || They can.
- Only when redundancy payments are made || Obligation exists earlier.
explain: An internal decision alone would not be enough.
mistake: Waiting for cash payments.
tip: Announced = expectation created.
src: Study text ch11 - constructive (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q032
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A company sold 4,000 products with a 1-year warranty. Expected outcomes: 90% no claim; 8% minor claim costing $25; 2% major claim costing $300.
q: What warranty provision ($) should be recognised?
answer: 32000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Provision = units x Σ(probability x cost)
step: Expected cost per unit = 0.08 x 25 + 0.02 x 300 = 2 + 6 = 8.
step: Provision = 4,000 x 8 = $32,000.
check: 4000*(0.08*25+0.02*300)
explain: Expected values suit a large population of items.
mistake: Using the most likely outcome (no claim).
tip: Weight costs by probability.
src: Study text ch11 - expected value (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO statements are correct under IAS 37?
+ Contingent liabilities are never recognised in the SOFP || They are disclosed or ignored.
+ A contingent asset is recognised only when its realisation is virtually certain || Then it is no longer contingent.
- Probable contingent assets are recognised || Disclosed only.
- Remote contingent liabilities must be disclosed || Ignored.
explain: Treatment is asymmetric between assets and liabilities.
mistake: Treating them the same way.
tip: Liabilities lean to disclosure; assets to caution.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t4
status: review
q: A trainee charges the full closing warranty provision of $18,000 to profit each year, even though $15,000 was brought forward. What is the error?
+ Only the movement should be charged; profit is understated || Double counting.
- No error || Double counting.
- Profit is overstated || Understated.
- The provision should be an asset || A liability.
explain: The opening provision was charged in earlier years.
mistake: Charging the balance again.
tip: Movement only.
src: Study text ch11 - movement (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
scenario: Payables: opening $21,400; credit purchases $164,000; cash paid $158,300; discounts received $2,700; returns $3,900; a contra of $1,600 with receivables.
q: What is the closing payables balance ($)?
answer: 18900
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: C/f = b/f + purchases - payments - discounts - returns - contras
step: 21,400 + 164,000 = 185,400.
step: 185,400 - 158,300 - 2,700 - 3,900 - 1,600 = $18,900.
check: 21400+164000-158300-2700-3900-1600
explain: Discounts received reduce the amount owed.
mistake: Leaving out the contra.
tip: Every reduction is a debit.
src: Study text ch11 - payables account (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q036
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: A company guarantees a bank loan for another company. At the year end the borrower is financially sound and default is unlikely but not remote. How is the guarantee treated?
+ Disclose a contingent liability || Possible outflow.
- Recognise a provision || Not probable.
- Ignore it || Not remote.
- Recognise a contingent asset || Not an asset.
explain: If default became probable, a provision would be needed.
mistake: Ignoring guarantees.
tip: Possible = note.
src: Study text ch11 - guarantees (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What does IAS 37 say about provisions where no reliable estimate is possible?
+ This is extremely rare; if it happens, the liability is disclosed as a contingent liability || Recognition fails.
- A provision of $1 is recognised || Not correct.
- The provision is recognised at the maximum possible amount || Not required.
- It is ignored || Disclosure is needed.
explain: A range of outcomes usually allows a reliable estimate.
mistake: Claiming estimates are impossible.
tip: Rarely impossible to estimate.
src: Study text ch11 - estimates (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A lawsuit is probable to be lost. The lawyers estimate a 60% chance of paying $100,000 and a 40% chance of paying $150,000. Following the best estimate of the single most likely outcome.
q: What provision ($) should be recognised?
answer: 100000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: For a single obligation, use the most likely outcome
step: Most likely outcome = 100,000 (60%).
step: Provision = $100,000.
check: 100000
explain: Expected values are used for large populations, such as warranties.
mistake: Using the weighted average (120,000) for a single case.
tip: Single case = most likely outcome.
src: Study text ch11 - measurement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How does recognising a new provision affect the financial statements?
+ Profit falls and liabilities rise by the same amount || Dr expense, Cr provision.
- Assets rise and profit rises || Not correct.
- Only cash falls || No cash yet.
- No effect until paid || Recognised when obligation exists.
explain: Cash leaves only when the obligation is settled.
mistake: Waiting for payment.
tip: Obligation now, cash later.
src: Study text ch11 - effect (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each probability with the treatment of a contingent ASSET.
pair: Virtually certain => Recognise the asset
pair: Probable => Disclose in a note
pair: Possible => Ignore
pair: Remote => Ignore
explain: Assets are treated more cautiously than liabilities.
mistake: Recognising probable assets.
tip: Only certainty recognises.
src: Study text ch11 - contingent assets (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: Provisions note: opening balance $24,000; amounts used during the year $7,500; unused amount reversed $3,000; new provisions charged $11,000.
q: What is the closing provision ($)?
answer: 24500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Closing = opening - used - reversed + new
step: 24,000 - 7,500 - 3,000 = 13,500.
step: 13,500 + 11,000 = $24,500.
check: 24000-7500-3000+11000
explain: The net P/L effect is 11,000 - 3,000 = 8,000.
mistake: Treating used amounts as reversals.
tip: Reconcile the movement.
src: Study text ch11 - movement note (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A board decides to retrain staff next year and wants to provide for the cost now. Is a provision allowed?
+ No - there is no present obligation from a past event; the company can still change its mind || Intention only.
- Yes - the cost is probable || No obligation.
- Yes - training is a legal obligation || Not here.
- Only half the cost || Not allowed.
explain: Future spending decisions are not obligations.
mistake: Providing for plans.
tip: Can avoid it = no provision.
src: Study text ch11 - no obligation (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Why does IAS 37 require strict criteria before recognising provisions?
+ To stop companies creating "hidden reserves" to smooth profits || Prevents manipulation.
- To increase reported profits || Not the aim.
- To delay recognising liabilities forever || Not the aim.
- To match tax rules || Not the reason.
explain: Over-provisioning in good years could be released in bad years.
mistake: Seeing provisions as optional buffers.
tip: No smoothing.
src: Study text ch11 - purpose (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t1
status: review
q: Credit purchases were $96,000 and cash paid to suppliers $88,500. Discounts received were $1,500 and opening payables were $9,000. What are closing payables ($)?
answer: 15000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: C/f = b/f + purchases - payments - discounts
step: 9,000 + 96,000 = 105,000.
step: 105,000 - 88,500 - 1,500 = $15,000.
check: 9000+96000-88500-1500
explain: Discounts received reduce the balance owed.
mistake: Ignoring discounts.
tip: Discounts are debits.
src: Study text ch11 - payables (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: After the year end, a court rules that the company must pay $80,000 in a case existing at the year end. At the year end the outcome had seemed only possible. What should be done?
+ Recognise a provision, as the ruling gives evidence about the condition at the year end || Adjusting evidence.
- Disclose only, as the year-end view governs || New evidence about the year end is used.
- Ignore it until next year || The obligation existed.
- Recognise a contingent asset || Not an asset.
explain: Events after the reporting period are covered in a later chapter.
mistake: Ignoring later evidence.
tip: Evidence about year-end conditions = adjust.
src: Study text ch11 - later evidence (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which item appears on the CREDIT side of the trade payables account?
+ Credit purchases || Increase the liability.
- Cash paid || Debit.
- Purchases returns || Debit.
- Discounts received || Debit.
explain: The opening balance is also a credit.
mistake: Crediting payments.
tip: Increases = credits.
src: Study text ch11 - payables account (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
scenario: A company must dismantle an oil platform in 10 years. The best estimate of the future cost is $2,000,000, and the present value of this cost today is $1,250,000.
q: What provision ($) is recognised now under IAS 37 (where the time value of money is material)?
answer: 1250000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Provision = present value of expected outflow when the time value of money is material
step: Future cost = 2,000,000; present value = 1,250,000.
step: Provision = $1,250,000.
check: 1250000
explain: The discount unwinds over time as a finance cost.
mistake: Providing the undiscounted amount.
tip: Material time value = discount.
src: Study text ch11 - discounting (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which BEST defines a contingent liability?
+ A possible obligation dependent on uncertain future events not wholly within the entity's control, or a present obligation that is not probable or cannot be measured reliably || Two-part definition.
- Any liability shown in the SOFP || Contingent liabilities are not recognised.
- A certain liability of known amount || That is a liability.
- An expense paid in advance || Prepayment.
explain: Contingent liabilities are disclosed unless remote.
mistake: Recognising them.
tip: Possible obligation = note.
src: Study text ch11 - contingent liability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: When must a provision be recognised?
+ When there is a present obligation from a past event, a probable outflow and a reliable estimate || All three conditions.
- Whenever directors want to be prudent || Not allowed.
- Only when the amount is known exactly || Estimates suffice.
- When an outflow is merely possible || Disclose instead.
explain: Missing any condition means no provision.
mistake: Providing for possible items.
tip: Obligation + probable + estimate.
src: Study text ch11 - recognition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: Which treatment is correct?
+ Possible contingent liability: disclose; probable contingent asset: disclose || Both disclosed.
- Possible contingent liability: provide || Disclose.
- Probable contingent asset: recognise || Disclose.
- Remote contingent liability: disclose || Ignore.
explain: Virtually certain items are no longer contingent.
mistake: Mixing up the tables.
tip: Learn both columns.
src: Study text ch11 - contingencies (concept basis; original question)
